---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How sparsh works — a quick tour"
  description="A quick, plain-language tour of how sparsh checks whether the target under a tap stayed put and kept its meaning."
>
  <main class="content-page explainer-page">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-dot" /> How sparsh works</p>
      <h1>Sometimes the page moves<br /><span>between “I meant that” and click.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">That little gap is where a good tap can go sideways. sparsh checks what’s under it: is it the thing the person saw, or did the UI sneak in something new?</p>
    </section>

    <section class="flow-section" aria-label="The sparsh decision flow">
      <div class="flow-step"><span>01</span><strong>You spot something</strong><p>You see Maya’s message and go to open it.</p></div>
      <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <div class="flow-step flow-change"><span>02</span><strong>The page does a thing</strong><p>A new message appears, or the row moves while you’re on your way.</p></div>
      <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <div class="flow-step flow-decision"><span>03</span><strong>sparsh checks the tap</strong><p>Does it still match what you were trying to do?</p></div>
    </section>

    <section class="belief-section" aria-labelledby="performance-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">BUILT TO STAY OUT OF THE WAY</p>
        <h2 id="performance-title">What does sparsh cost?</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="belief-copy">
        <p>
          sparsh checks an activation as it happens. It uses a small set of shared event listeners
          and does work for the interaction, rather than keeping a registry of every control in your
          app.
        </p>
        <p>
          We aim to keep that extra work below one millisecond per interaction and measure DOM
          observer cost with a virtualized 10,000-row interface. These are performance targets, not
          published benchmark results yet. <a href="https://github.com/ashfaqc137/sparsh#performance">See the detailed performance plan.</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-section">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">FOUR WAYS A TAP CAN GO SIDEWAYS</p>
        <h2>What does sparsh check?</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-grid">
        <article class="policy-card">
          <span class="policy-icon">◷</span><p class="policy-name">AGE</p><h3>Did it just show up?</h3>
          <p><strong>Age</strong> checks how long something has been visible. A brand-new message that pops up under a tap may not be what the person meant to open.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="policy-card">
          <span class="policy-icon">⌖</span><p class="policy-name">CONTINUITY</p><h3>Did it move while you pressed?</h3>
          <p><strong>Continuity</strong> checks whether the same thing stayed in the same place from pointer down to pointer up. It catches a layout shift or a new control taking over.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="policy-card">
          <span class="policy-icon">◈</span><p class="policy-name">SEMANTICS</p><h3>Did the button’s job change?</h3>
          <p><strong>Semantics</strong> checks things like disabled state, accessible label, link or input value, and an optional <code>data-guard-key</code>.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="policy-card">
          <span class="policy-icon">↻</span><p class="policy-name">DOUBLE FIRE</p><h3>Did the last click uncover something?</h3>
          <p><strong>Double fire</strong> watches for a quick second activation on a different target after the first action changed or collapsed the UI.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="report-panel">
      <div class="report-mark" aria-hidden="true">◎</div>
      <div>
        <p class="section-kicker">DEFAULT PROTECTION</p>
        <h2>Suspicious taps are stopped.</h2>
        <p>By default, sparsh runs in <code>enforce</code> mode and stops suspicious activations. You can switch to <code>report</code> mode to observe decisions without blocking, or set modes per policy.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mode-pills"><span>enforce <small>default</small></span><span>report <small>observe only</small></span></div>
    </section>

    <section class="accessibility-note">
      <span class="note-icon" aria-hidden="true">⌘</span>
      <div><h2>Keyboard users count too.</h2><p>Keyboard and assistive-technology activations are handled separately. Continuity only runs when there’s an actual pointer press to compare. Escape, moving focus, and scrolling are never blocked.</p></div>
    </section>

    <section class="limits-note">
      <p class="section-kicker">A QUICK REALITY CHECK</p>
      <p>sparsh would rather miss a tricky case than block a tap it can’t explain. It doesn’t catch every visual change yet—opacity-only reveals and some overlays can slip by. <a href="/docs/concepts/#limits">See the full policy behavior and known limits.</a></p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
